---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/pipeline.json';

const { priceability } = data;
const n = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en-GB');

const supplies = [
  ['Arrival at the anchorage', 'Notice of Readiness — an email, not a physical event'],
  ['Berthing, to the minute', 'Hoses on and off; a tanker lies alongside for hours before pumping'],
  ['Departure', 'Free pratique, customs clearance'],
  ['Waiting versus working hours', 'The charter party terms themselves'],
];
---
<Base
  title="How laytime is counted — what AIS supplies, and what it cannot"
  description="Every hour in a laytime statement belongs to exactly one line with a reason attached. This is where those lines come from, and where AIS stops being able to help."
>
  <h1>How laytime is counted here</h1>

  <p class="lede prose">
    A total nobody can decompose is a total nobody can dispute — and disputing it is the point. So
    every hour between commencement and completion belongs to exactly one line, and every line says
    why.
  </p>

  <h2>The four kinds of hour</h2>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>line</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>BeforeCommencement</code></td>
          <td>Time examined and not charged — turn time, or waiting before notice took effect.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Counted</code></td>
          <td>Laytime running against the allowance.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Excepted</code></td>
          <td>Time the charter party excludes. An input, not something AIS can detect.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>OnDemurrage</code></td>
          <td>The allowance is spent; this is what the rate is applied to.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">
    The lines are asserted to sum back to the elapsed time — the same accounting identity the
    ingest applies to rows, where every row read is either inserted, a duplicate, quarantined or
    filtered, and the counters must balance or the run fails.
  </p>

  <h2>What AIS supplies, and what it cannot</h2>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>AIS supplies</th><th>AIS cannot supply</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {supplies.map(([can, cannot]) => (
          <tr><td>{can}</td><td>{cannot}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="prose">
    <p>
      <strong>AIS cannot compute demurrage</strong>, and the output says so rather than quietly
      filling the gap. Notice of Readiness is an email. Hoses on and off happen hours after
      berthing. Free pratique is a document. What AIS supplies is precisely the half a Statement of
      Facts is least able to prove: where the vessel physically was, and when it stopped moving.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p class="prose">
    Where a notice time is not supplied, arrival is substituted and the statement <em>labels that
    assumption</em> rather than presenting it as observed. A figure resting on an assumption and a
    figure resting on a measurement are different figures, and a reader is entitled to know which
    one they are holding.
  </p>

  <h2>When it declines</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    Of {n(priceability.callsAssessed)} port calls in this window,
    {' '}<strong>{n(priceability.priceable)}</strong> can be priced.
    {' '}{n(priceability.noBerthPhase)} cannot, because the vessel never went alongside — there
    were no cargo operations to measure, and inventing a berth time from an anchorage would put a
    fabricated timestamp into a commercial claim. A further
    {' '}{n(priceability.berthGeometryUntrustworthy)} have hours inside the berth span whose
    geometry the pipeline does not stand behind.
  </p>

  <p class="prose">
    That last refusal is the one worth understanding. Excluding uncertain hours favours the
    charterer; counting them favours the owner. Neither is supportable from the evidence, so no
    figure is produced at all and the hours in question are named — leaving the judgement to a
    human who has more than a radio signal to go on.
  </p>

  <h2>The terms are yours, not ours</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    Allowed hours, the demurrage rate, turn time and any exceptions are <em>inputs</em>. Anything
    quoted on this site as an example uses illustrative tanker defaults — plausible numbers chosen
    for a demonstration, never from a real charter party. The engine is correct for the terms it is
    given; supplying the real ones is why the tool runs on your machine.
  </p>
</Base>
